+"""Experimental, inference-only Qwen MoE expert streaming from SSD, without Accelerate or native I/O dependencies.
+
+Supported checkpoints: ``qwen3_moe``, ``olmoe`` and the text model of ``qwen3_5_moe`` (Qwen3.5/3.6 MoE), unquantized.
+The experts can instead come from a llama.cpp GGUF of the same model (Q8_0, F16, BF16 or F32 expert tensors), so that
+the streamed bytes match what llama.cpp-based engines read; dense weights still come from the safetensors checkpoint.
+
+How it works
+============
+
+1. The hook Transformers already has.
+   In Qwen3 MoE and Qwen3.5/3.6 MoE, each layer's experts live in one ``Experts`` module as two packed tensors,
+   ``gate_up_proj`` ``[experts, 2 * intermediate, hidden]`` and ``down_proj`` ``[experts, hidden, intermediate]``.
+   The router produces ``top_k_index`` / ``top_k_weights``, then the experts module calls whatever function is
+   registered under ``config._experts_implementation`` through ``ExpertsInterface`` (the same kind of registry as
+   attention). No model code is patched: this module registers ``"ssd_streaming"`` and points the config at it.
+
+2. Loading without the experts in memory (``load_moe_from_ssd``).
+   - Build the model on the ``meta`` device: nothing is allocated.
+   - For every experts module, delete the two big parameters (``register_parameter(..., None)``) and attach the
+     shared ``ExpertStore`` plus the module's checkpoint prefix (e.g. ``model.language_model.layers.12.mlp.experts``).
+   - Load everything else (attention, linear attention, router, shared expert, embeddings, norms) one tensor at a
+     time, straight to the device.
+   - Check that every checkpoint tensor was either loaded, deliberately left on disk (experts), or skipped (vision
+     tower, MTP head).
+
+3. Reading one expert (``SafetensorsReader`` / ``GGUFExpertReader``).
+   - safetensors: the header gives each tensor's byte offset; in a packed bank expert ``e`` is one contiguous slice at
+     ``offset + e * prod(shape[1:]) * itemsize``. One ``readinto`` fills a buffer, ``torch.frombuffer`` wraps it
+     without a copy, ``.to(device)`` uploads it. A whole bank or shard is never read.
+   - GGUF: a small header parser finds ``blk.N.ffn_{gate,up,down}_exps``. ggml stores those as
+     ``[columns, rows, experts]``, so one expert is again a contiguous ``rows x columns`` matrix in the PyTorch
+     ``(out_features, in_features)`` orientation. A Q8_0 row is blocks of 34 bytes (one float16 scale and 32 int8
+     values), so a Qwen3.6 expert is 3.2 MiB instead of 6 MiB in BF16.
+
+4. The cache (``ExpertStore``).
+   One global LRU across all layers, keyed by ``(layer prefix, expert id)`` and budgeted in bytes. A miss evicts the
+   least recently used experts until the new one fits, reads it, uploads it and inserts it. In GGUF mode the cache
+   holds the GGUF's own bytes (e.g. Q8_0) and ``dequantize_gguf`` turns them into the execution dtype on the device
+   each time the expert runs, so the same budget holds about twice as many Q8_0 experts as BF16 ones.
+
+5. The forward pass (``streamed_experts_forward``)::
+
+       routing = top_k_index.cpu()                      # one small device-to-host copy per layer
+       for expert in unique(routing):
+           tokens, slots = where(routing == expert)
+           gate_up, down = store.get(layer, expert)     # cache hit, or read from the SSD
+           for each chunk of at most token_chunk_size tokens:   # bounds prefill memory
+               gate, up = linear(hidden[tokens], gate_up).chunk(2)
+               out = linear(act(gate) * up, down) * top_k_weights[tokens, slots]
+               result.index_add_(0, tokens, out)
+
+   This is the same math as the eager experts. The tests compare logits and greedy generations with the eager model
+   on tiny Qwen3 MoE and Qwen3.5 MoE checkpoints (packed and per-expert layouts, forced evictions, zero cache, and the
+   Q8_0 GGUF path).
+
+6. Where the time goes.
+   Measured on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B Q8_0 with an A10G limited to 12 GiB of GPU memory and 12 GiB of RAM: each decoded token
+   makes 40 layers x 8 = 320 expert lookups; with a 5.5 GiB cache and a 77% hit rate that is about 75 misses, about
+   250 MB read per token. At 2.2 tok/s that is about 0.55 GB/s, far below what the disk delivers (5 GB/s direct).
+   The limits are elsewhere: reads are synchronous (the GPU waits on every miss, nothing is prefetched), there is a
+   Python loop over experts in every layer, and Q8_0 is dequantized on every use. The next steps would be reading
+   layer N+1's experts while layer N computes, a grouped MoE kernel instead of the loop, and a Q8_0 matmul.
+
+7. What it deliberately is not.
+   Inference only, one request at a time, a single device, not wired into ``from_pretrained``. By default reads use
+   ordinary buffered file I/O, so the OS page cache can help and ``read_bytes`` counts requested bytes, NOT physical
+   SSD traffic; ``bypass_page_cache=True`` keeps read bytes out of the page cache, so every miss reads the storage. Dense weights stay in the execution dtype even when the experts come from a GGUF.
+"""
+
+import heapq
+import json
+import math
+import os
+import re
+import struct
+import threading
+from collections import OrderedDict
+from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
+from pathlib import Path
+
+import torch
+from safetensors import safe_open
+
+
+try:  # POSIX only; used to keep streamed bytes out of the page cache on macOS (F_NOCACHE)
+    import fcntl
+except ImportError:  # Windows
+    fcntl = None
+
